From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Wed, 27 Jul 94 12:08 CDT
Subject: [#94-7-228] Innateness of language [was If Knowledge Limits Learning]

In <9407271517.aa04997@genie.genie.slhs.udel.edu>, nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu writes:
> Carlf says that chimps have no grasp of syntax--I wonder if there's a
> possibility that they have a different system of systax.

I missed the original, but my take on the chimps-learning-language
business is that they've never gotten to the point of using
relationships complex enough to require syntax, e.g., third-person
observations such as "the dog bites the man", "the man the dog bites",
"Canis homo masticat" (terrible latin warning :-), or even, given the
ability to indicate "cup", say "I drink from the cup", "I use the
cup".

Even the gorilla Koko, patiently trained from infancy, to the point
where many critics doubt that the project means much anymore, says
(signs) things like "fine animal gorilla" as her most complex (and
egotistical) utterance.

So, maybe some apes are "wired" for syntax in some way that we haven't
yet triggered, but I don't see why they would be when they don't have
language yet.

-- cP

