From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 27 Jul 94 18:54:52 EDT
Subject: [#94-7-234] FAQ & medical ethics 

Marvin Minsky says:
>Yup. In the late 60s there was a highly promising  brain-implanted visual
>prosthesis -- but little has happened in this field since then, because of
>"ethics."  Look at the book, @i[Visual Prosthesis,] Academic  Press, 1971,
>pp. 315-319.  

Another approach to this problem was taken by researcher Paul Bach-y-Rita
reported in 1972.  (Described in Daniel Dennett, Consciousness Explained pg
338-342.)  A low-resolution video camera was mounted to an eyeglass frame.
The low-resolution signal from the camera was used to generate a 16-by-16 or
20-by-20 array of on-off signals.  The signals were feed into a grid of
electrical or mechanical vibrating tinglers called tactors which were placed
on the subjects back or belly.  After training, blind subjects could read
signs, identify objects and even people's faces based on the stimuli they
received from their skin.  Dennett suggests a similarity to interpreting
letters traced on the skin with a fingertip.

Thus, this non-invasive approach apparently still allowed subjects to
experience a sensation in some ways similar to a crude version of vision.
Dennett relates the intriguing fact that subjects who had been trained with
the tingler-patch on their backs could adapt almost immediately when the
tingler-patch was shifted to their bellies.  The experiment is interesting
philosophically because it forces one to think about the coherence of
some notions of `qualia'.

> Because of the lawyers and ethicists, no such experiment could be 
>done now, or (?) ever again.  The lady actually volunteered for the 
>experiment because of her long interest in neuroscience!  But no hospital 
>would now cooperate, I think. 

Approval of elective surgery for implanting experimental devices in the
brain probably is harder to obtain now.  However, I do not know what group
would try to stop the skin oriented work described above from being
extended. Dennett does not describe any recent advances.  Higher resolution
arrays for the tingler-patches and more distinct levels of stimulation at
each `pixel' are natural extensions.  Of course, the approach may be
ultimately limited by the neural properties of the skin on the back and
belly. The approach might also be limited by some kind of restrictions on
the brain's adaptiveness.

Finally, some `radical' (in my opinion) experiments have been allowed by
ethics panels in the recent past, e.g., the implantation of a baboon heart in
a human.

