Leon Llouw of South Africa's Free Market Foundation argues that where
there are large deposits of easily exploited natural resources there is
a tendency for more statist forms of government to occur than where such
deposits are absent.  Venezuela, Nigeria, Saudi Arabia, and other oil-
reliant countries seem to confirm this, as do places like Hong Kong and
Switzerland, with little natural resources, highly free economies, and
much prosperity.

I've heard it argued that Sweden is also such a corporatist "company town,"
dominated by Volvo.

>What parts of the United States (beyond Louisiana, which in a way is
>the most obvious example I can think of, and before you ask, no, I
>don't think I'd change that answer if I didn't live here) are most
>like Venezuela in relying on one or a few industries for there
>economic liveleihood?

Alaska and Texas for oil, Nevada for gaming, LA for the film industry,
New York for the financial industry, Washington D.C. for the extortion
industry...

>...Is what is happening in Venezuela a possible "dress-rehersal" for the
>United States? (This question could also apply to the last 40 years of
>the history of the Phillipines).

I doubt it.  Socialism seems to be on the run in the Northern hemisphere,
only making progress in the Southern (and not all of that).  Latin America
has had some recent gains and setbacks for socialism: Venezuela, Chiapas
Mexico, etc., but also Chile, Peru, El Salvador, etc.  And it just made a
big gain in South Africa, but perhaps not in Ruwanda.

