Subject: [#94-7-237] Books for Resource List

I've been reading some history books lately that Extropians might find
interesting, starting with 3 by William H. McNeill, professor emeritus of
history at the University of Chicago: "The Rise of the West: a History of
the Human Community," which takes as its theme the interaction between
different cultures, especially the transmission of skills and technologies
between them and the social changes that resulted, "Plagues and Peoples,"
which pretty much does the same thing for germs, viruses, and other "micro-
parasites," as he calls them, and "The Pursuit of Power," which does the
same thing for military skills & technologies since 1000 A.D.  They're all
very readable, original in many ways (such as being able to explain the
economics of why ancient Greece & Rome were different from Egypt and Persia),
and go together well.  They give a good overview of some of the materialistic
forces and trends that have operated (and may still be operating) in human
history.

"The Washing of the Spears: the Rise and Fall of the Zulu Nation" by Donald
R. Morris is also interesting, as an examination of the various social forces
operating in the 19th century, how they conflicted and cooperated, in micro-
cosm.  See how one of the few States that actually was formed within historical
time from a nomadic society!  How the Boers and the British Empire reacted!
Who the good guys and bad guys might really be!  It's a bit dry in places, but
well worth plowing through.

What also might be worth putting on the list are James Burke's "Connections"
videos/books as well as some of his other works.  Does anyone know where they
can be rented?  I just found a number they can be ordered from, but they're
too rich for my blood, about $179.00.  Some of the best popularization of the
history of technology I've ever seen in any form.  I'll post the number when
I have it handy.

